  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on social media comments from a single Chinese city, which may reflect localized regulatory conditions, platform demographics, and cultural attitudes toward autonomous driving.",
      "what": "The study analyzed over 170,000 public comments regarding the Apollo Go robotaxi service in Wuhan, China, using a RoBERTa classifier and Sentence-BERT embeddings. It categorized public sentiment and discourse patterns based on three distinct situational roles: passengers, pedestrians, and motorists. The analysis revealed that while passengers generally report positive experiences, their routine use is hindered by operational boundaries and usability frictions, whereas pedestrians and motorists express significant concerns regarding safety, traffic unpredictability, and congestion.",
      "why": "It provides a scalable, digital-trace methodology to capture real-world user and non-user feedback under actual operating conditions, moving beyond traditional survey methods to pinpoint specific integration bottlenecks for autonomous vehicles in dense urban environments."
    }
  },
